NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"pr-2078-origin-attribution-and-refused-insert-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR #2078: origin attribution and refused insert review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review target: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a4e6acd33dbb41ca220541ef21414efb3fbbb5ce"},{"type":"text","value":" against\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2c98b4992c412820f8476325ee6941db5ca9ac0f"},{"type":"text","value":". The eight external findings\nwere supplied in source order. The repair and executable oracle revision is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"43608b4f94a845de3e8d8be2373c42b0ab65efb7"},{"type":"text","value":". This record follows that\ncommit; it does not change its executable code. The private issue #2071 bridge\nhistory remains unavailable and is not treated as refuted."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"finding-ledger","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding ledger"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed claim"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence and technical verdict"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition and durable destination"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F01"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$origin"},{"type":"text","value":" text omits pending transactions; a same-key source row can stay local after pending rollback, without truncate."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"True. A manual pending transaction does not hold source commits. Four controlled cells cross optimistic visibility and fulfillment/rollback, and compare origin at source publication and settlement."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": API comments, glossary, guide, both reference pages, and the primary optimistic-history model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F02"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingLocalChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" says persisting, but includes pending."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"True by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"overlayActiveTransactions()"},{"type":"text","value":" state predicate and F01's public witness."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": production comment names pending and persisting."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F03"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate's retained origin leaks to a later same-key transaction in the same drain."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"True product bug. The new reentrant history was RED on the reviewed production code: at failed-mutation settlement, the model expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remote"},{"type":"text","value":" and the public row was "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"local"},{"type":"text","value":". A one-transaction control passed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": scope retained snapshots to each sync transaction in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"; the same oracle is GREEN."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F04"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The source-batch model cannot express delete then reinsert within one transaction, and its opening omits that limit."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"True on the reviewed head. The grammar now preserves mixed source operation order, and the opening states the default and ordered lanes."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": primary model/driver and atomic versus later-transaction witnesses."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F05"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The real SQLite test claims an update but writes an insert."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"True. The original continuation had insert, delete, insert. It now writes a real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" and checks adapter forwarding, live/base/durable rows, and reopen."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": real adapter receiving witness and accurate comment."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F06"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanupRealWitness"},{"type":"text","value":" duplicates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanupPersistedOracle"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Substantially true shared loop, though the latter also enforces a 250 ms limit per action."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"test-cleanup.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" helper; the persisted owner keeps its timeout wrapper."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F07"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first Collection is cleaned twice; a failure may remove the directory before resources settle."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The double call was true. The claimed open SQLite handle was not established: this witness uses a CLI driver with one process per operation. The old finalizer still attempted directory removal after cleanup failure."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":": track both Collections' successful cleanup and remove the directory only after both release; retain the primary failure and cleanup diagnostics."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F08"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guide's timing rules leave overlapping same-key mutations unspecified while the oracle stays green."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coverage gap is true. The guide already qualified its first-transaction rule to one mutation without truncate, so it did not promise every overlap. Sixteen bounded histories now cross two active same-key mutations, one/two queued source transactions, both settlement orders, and all success/failure pairs."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that bounded gap: guide, model, publication driver, and coverage map. Broader overlap schedules remain explicitly outside the closure claim."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original review had eight items. All eight received an in-PR action. F07's\nopen-handle mechanism and F08's claim of an unqualified guide promise were\noverstated; those qualifications do not erase their useful findings."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"laws-and-enforcement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Laws and enforcement"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"l1-active-local-keys-and-pending-settlement-f01-f02","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L1. Active local keys and pending settlement (F01, F02)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: the key-and-timing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$origin"},{"type":"text","value":" contract in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"virtual-props.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and the\nglossary. A pending manual local transaction is active before its mutation\nfunction starts. A same-key source commit applies immediately, may receive\nlocal attribution, and keeps that origin after the manual transaction rolls\nback. The source identity is unknown. The old model had only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":", so its grammar could not reach the reported cut."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model now distinguishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":": the same source commit\napplies immediately in the former state and queues in the latter. The driver\nuses public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutate"},{"type":"text","value":", sync writes,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollback"},{"type":"text","value":", and Collection reads. Four cells cross optimistic\nvisibility and success/failure. After each step, the shared driver compares\npublic rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request\noutcomes, and complete publication cuts. A temporary production mutant that\nrequired "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasPersistingTransaction()"},{"type":"text","value":" before consulting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingLocalChanges"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreached the source and settlement cuts and failed all four cells. It was\nrestored before the executable commit. This establishes the bounded manual\none-key path, not every pending and persisting overlap."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"l2-truncate-attribution-ends-with-its-own-source-transaction-f03","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L2. Truncate attribution ends with its own source transaction (F03)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: the existing per-atomic-transaction attribution contract and the\ntruncate comment in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". A subscriber can submit a truncate and a later\nsame-key source transaction while core drains an earlier truncate. Those\naccepted suffix transactions apply in source order during the next drain.\nAttribution retained for the truncate must not color the later transaction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The old driver could only commit one source batch at a time, so a truncate\ndrained before it could queue a successor in the same drain. The new reentrant\nstep commits both from a real Collection subscriber. The independent model\nqueues the suffix and snapshots attribution for each batch. The one-batch\ncontrol expects local attribution; the two-batch history expects remote for\nthe later row. On the original production branch, the second history failed\nat the public row observed when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" rejected: expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remote"},{"type":"text","value":", got\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"local"},{"type":"text","value":". Moving the retained sets inside the transaction loop made both\nhistories pass. The driver also checks event replicas and downstream state.\nCoverage is one active mutation, two truncates, and zero or one later same-key\ntransaction; arbitrary reentrant suffixes are not claimed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"l3-ordered-operations-retain-one-atomic-origin-f04","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L3. Ordered operations retain one atomic origin (F04)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: a sync transaction is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"begin()"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" atomic batch, and\nthe existing first-transaction origin law. The model's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":" action is a\ngrammar abstraction over source insert/update; the driver selects the source\nwrite kind from its own key membership. The model does not import that driver\nclassifier. Ordered operations now represent delete then reinsert in one\nbatch. The model snapshots origin at batch start, consumes it for later\ntransactions, and derives expected rows independently of production caches."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fixed witness reaches the real sync path with delete then reinsert of an\nexisting key. A neighboring history adds a later same-key transaction. Public\norigin at successful settlement is local in the atomic-only case and remote\nafter the later transaction. A temporary production mutant that omitted a\ndelete from the batch-local attribution set failed the atomic-only witness at\nsettlement, while the later-transaction control passed. The ordered grammar\nrejects mixed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"operations"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rows"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deletes"},{"type":"text","value":" and more than one copy. Other\nmixed operation sequences are representable but not exhaustively sampled."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"l4-successful-overlapping-mutations-preserve-one-key-grant-f08","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L4. Successful overlapping mutations preserve one key grant (F08)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: queued source attribution is by key and timing, not by source\nauthor or one grant per optimistic transaction. If two same-key mutations are\nboth persisting before a source transaction queues, a successful mutation\nretains one attribution for the key even if its sibling fails. Two failures\nretain none. The first same-key source transaction consumes that grant; a\nsecond source transaction is remote without another local owner."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model already represented multiple persisting transactions but had no\ndistinguishing overlap campaign. Sixteen bounded histories now cross the two\noutcomes, settlement order, and one/two source transactions. The core driver\ncompares public origin at each settlement, full event cuts, and downstream\nrows. A temporary production mutant clearing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingLocalOrigins"},{"type":"text","value":" whenever a\nfailed sibling remained failed four one-success/one-source cells at the\nsettlement read, while two-failure controls passed. The tested histories do\nnot include pending plus persisting overlap, multiple keys, truncate during\noverlap, or all possible handler schedules. The coverage map retains those\nlimits; no universal overlap proof is claimed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"l5-real-sqlite-receives-source-updates-after-refusal-f05-f07","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L5. Real SQLite receives source updates after refusal (F05, F07)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: accepted source transactions must reach the Collection and its\ndurable persisted replica; a rejected local insert contributes no durable row.\nThe real SQLite witness holds outbound rejection, accepts a same-key delete,\nthen follows one of two histories. The continuation now inserts, deletes,\nreinserts, and updates through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncConfig.write"},{"type":"text","value":". It checks live and exposed\nbase rows, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"applyCommittedTx"},{"type":"text","value":"'s update mutation, durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":", and a\nfresh Collection/adapter/driver reopened over the same file. A temporary\nadapter mutant dropped only the final update before durable application. It\nreached the new update cut and failed the durable-row comparison: SQLite kept\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"later source row"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updated source row"},{"type":"text","value":". The original broad mutant\nalso dropped an earlier source turn and failed too early; that result is not\ncounted as update calibration. The receiving witness uses a SQLite CLI driver\nand a same-process reopen; other hosts and a process restart remain outside."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-guide-check","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle-guide check"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L1–L5 name the contract and finite path limits. The private bridge remains unknown."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model derives rows and origin from source actions and key ownership, without production caches or classifiers. The shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceOperations"},{"type":"text","value":" function only expands grammar order."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opening prose states laws and limits; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HistoryModel"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OptimisticStep"},{"type":"text","value":", the real Collection driver, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"check()"},{"type":"text","value":" keep model, grammar, path, and refinement distinct."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pending versus persisting, one versus two queued source transactions, batch versus transaction boundary, and both settlement orders have reconstructing witnesses. Bounded domains are above. The driver rejects an ordered batch mixed with legacy rows/deletes or multiple copies, and a reentrant trigger without truncate. Other legal schedules remain listed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Public Collection writes, sync callbacks, subscription replicas, downstream rows, receipts, and settlement-time reads reach the named cuts. The real adapter path checks durable and reopened rows."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original production code and four targeted hostile edits fail assertions at the intended public cuts. The too-broad SQLite edit failed early and is excluded from the update claim. No timeout or setup error is counted as a kill."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 fixed/random parity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered by the new bounded enumerations and fixed histories. Existing generated campaigns in the owner retain their prior configuration."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pending differs from persisting because sync applies now versus waits. Source operation order and a combined reentrant drain change legal next observations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 vocabulary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":", sync transaction, publication, and settlement match the glossary. The model-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":" action combines source insert/update as stated in the opening."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 failure fidelity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core owner keeps its primary failure through cleanup. The SQLite owners now share cleanup aggregation; the persisted owner still bounds each stage, and the real witness releases Collections before directory removal."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Atomic delete/reinsert versus a later transaction challenges batch-boundary semantics. Causal source authorship cannot be independently decided without a source identity signal; no such guarantee is claimed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record binds original head, executable revision, RED/GREEN, mutants, finite closure, and remaining cells."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 distinguishing boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One versus two source transactions, one versus two reentrant suffix batches, pending versus persisting, and one/two overlapping outcomes distinguish plausible wrong timing rules at public cuts."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 controlled handoff"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The real SQLite receiver tests the controlled refused-insert and source-update premise. The core reentrant history is a core claim, not claimed as real-provider coverage."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification-and-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification and limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the executable revision, 166 core optimistic history and state-retention\ntests passed; the commit-hook rerun passed 61 core publication and outcome tests.\nThe relevant persisted and real SQLite run passed 18 tests, with unrelated\npersisted cases filtered. Vitest's test type checks passed. Prettier and the\nchanged-file ESLint run had no errors; ESLint reported pre-existing warnings\nin the large persisted oracle, and one new shadow warning was repaired before\ncommit. Git's commit hook ran ESLint fixes; the two focused suites were rerun\nafter the commit. The production diff in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" is seven added and six\nremoved lines, including the corrected comment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The issue #2071 bridge sequence is still unavailable. This review does not\nclaim to close its private path, every overlap schedule, every mixed source\nbatch, another SQLite host, or process restart. The optimistic-history owner\ncan represent mixed source order and bounded reentry; a bridge-specific\ncounterexample needs the callback/write sequence and a persisted-wrapper\nreceiving witness. The coverage map names those limits. No authorized in-scope\nrepair from these eight findings is left open."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final accounting: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8 raw items = 8 fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":". F07 and F08 include the\ntechnical qualifications recorded above; neither was dropped from the ledger."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"post-audit-ci-and-review-follow-ups","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Post-audit CI and review follow-ups"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"db-2"},{"type":"text","value":" CI shard on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"00fcf0f"},{"type":"text","value":" passed 5,901 runtime tests but reported a\ntest type error at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic-history-oracle.ts:987"},{"type":"text","value":": the new manual\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction<HistoryRow>"},{"type":"text","value":" was missing from the driver's transaction union.\nCommit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9cb0e9998ba7fc4f8bb67f2ddc6c45e58717c24b"},{"type":"text","value":" adds that type. The\npackage test TypeScript check and 42 origin-publication tests, including their\nVitest type check, pass locally. This change does not alter the model or\nproduction behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit then reviewed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"00fcf0f"},{"type":"text","value":" and posted two actionable documentation\ncomments. Both were correct. The earlier issue #2071 review record used present\ntense for the source-batch grammar's former delete/reinsert limit; its affected\nstatements now name the historical commit and point to the ordered-batch witness\nadded in this PR. The generated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualRowProps"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" reference\npages linked to old source line numbers; all seven declaration links now match\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"virtual-props.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". These follow-ups do not change the eight-item accounting\nabove. All reported CI checks passed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit's next review, "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5459093045","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5459093045"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\nchecked that head and found an oracle harness defect. Its three findings across\nboth reviews are accounted for separately from the user's eight findings:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verdict and evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR action and durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The earlier #2071 review record still claimed that delete then reinsert was outside the source-batch grammar."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"00fcf0f"},{"type":"text","value":"; the ordered grammar and coverage-map witness existed by that commit."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e"},{"type":"text","value":"; historical wording now identifies the earlier limit and later witness."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Six "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualRowProps"},{"type":"text","value":" anchors and one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" anchor pointed into source comments."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"00fcf0f"},{"type":"text","value":"; all seven current anchors match the declarations at lines 74, 85, 104, 133, 141, 149, and 36."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e"},{"type":"text","value":"; generated reference links were corrected."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A rejected pending manual transaction never starts its handler, so its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" promise and oracle cleanup can remain pending. Commit the transaction after rejecting its handler promise."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e"},{"type":"text","value":". Two source-row histories and one later-edit history failed at the settlement row assertion after the test first made cleanup safe. The proposed single-line fix alone leaves a later-edit history pending because the shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"starting"},{"type":"text","value":" variable points to the later request."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in this follow-up: commit the rejected manual request, bind each manual handler to its own deferred result, and release any still-active transaction during cleanup. The primary oracle and coverage map retain the law and limits."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reviewer's three findings were accurate and focused. The proposed fix for\nCR3 identified the missing commit but did not account for a later edit replacing\nthe shared deferred promise. The reviews distinguished documentation defects\nfrom a test-integrity defect and had little noise. Adjacent interleavings\nstill need checking before accepting the proposed patch verbatim."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cr3-law-reach-and-calibration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR3 law, reach, and calibration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A pending manual optimistic transaction starts its mutation function only when\ncommitted. Handler rejection must roll it back, reject "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" with the\nsame reason, and expose the applied source row after dropping any optimistic\noverlay. This follows from the optimistic transaction and settlement entries in\nthe glossary and from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction.commit()"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollback()"},{"type":"text","value":". The production path\nis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" → the\ncontrolled handler promise → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":". The model already represented\npending, persisting, and failed transactions. Its grammar could select a\nrejection but the pending fixed witnesses selected only success or direct\nrollback. The driver did not commit on rejection, and cleanup tried rollback\nonly when the expected result was still pending."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The expanded fixed matrix crosses optimistic visibility with success, direct\nrollback, and handler rejection after a same-key source insert. A second pair\nstarts a later disjoint edit before the manual request succeeds or rejects.\nAfter each step, the driver checks public rows, origin and pending-write\nmetadata, subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request outcome and reason,\nand publication cuts. At settlement it also checks the rows captured when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" settles. The source-row and later-edit rejection cases failed at\nthat settlement assertion on the reviewed harness. They were assertion\nfailures, not Vitest timeouts; cleanup preserved the primary mismatch and\nreleased the pending transaction. With the commit fix in place, temporarily\nrestoring the shared handler made both later-edit cases fail at the same\nassertion. Binding the manual handler to its own deferred result makes all\neight targeted cases pass. This calibrates both the original omission and the\nplausible one-line repair that misses an adjacent legal history."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This establishes the bounded law for the named manual histories through the\ncore Collection path and public settlement, row, and publication checkpoints.\nIt does not prove longer manual interleavings, same-key cascades, or a persisted\nadapter path. The coverage map keeps those limits under the optimistic history\nowner. CR3 changes the oracle driver and its fixed histories, not the product\nimplementation or model state."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR3 evidence"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001/003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle's opening states the manual settlement law and limits; the model, grammar, driver, and per-step comparison remain distinct."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reference transition remains independent of transaction implementation; no expected state was copied from the new driver."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004/007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The new cases are bounded fixed histories; generated grammar and fixed/random campaigns did not change."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005/006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real core transactions reach "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" and public rows. Original and shared-handler variants fail at the intended settlement assertion; the repaired driver passes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008/009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model state did not change. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":", and settlement retain glossary meanings."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup rolls back a still-active transaction regardless of expected outcome; the original assertion remains the aggregate cause, with secondary diagnostics retained. The deferred rejection has a handler before a manual commit starts."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No plausible shared semantic fault between production and model was identified; this review concerned the production driver's ability to reach the modeled outcome."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012/013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record binds the reviewed head, RED/GREEN cuts, hostile variant, neighboring success/rollback cases, and remaining limits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The claim is limited to the core Collection path and makes no real-provider handoff claim."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final CodeRabbit accounting: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3 raw findings = 3 fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":". There are no\ndeferred or design-decision items. The original user's eight-item audit remains\n"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8 raw = 8 fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; the unknown private #2071 bridge sequence remains an\nevidence limit, not a CodeRabbit item."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"subsequent-nine-finding-review-of-e659d413e","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subsequent nine-finding review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The external medium-effort review examined "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3"},{"type":"text","value":"\nby reading code; it reported no executed checks. Evaluation began on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797eb7cfc9dfa18a05637477a10de67828"},{"type":"text","value":", which had already repaired\nthe manual-transaction defects in M04 and M05. The nine numbered findings are\nall the review's claims; its closing remarks add context, not a tenth item."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review claim and proposed repair"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Technical verdict and evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR disposition and durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M01"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate on key 1 clears active attribution for untouched key 2; a later key 2 source transaction in the same drain becomes remote."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct, high severity."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A real subscriber committed a truncate suffix and later key 2 write while an edit on key 2 remained active. The public row was remote where the independent model required local. Both publication and settlement variants failed on the reviewed production logic."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Preserve active keys across truncate; the primary optimistic-history oracle owns the origin law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M02"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clearing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingLocalChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" on truncate caused M01; keep it and remove the temporary origin snapshots."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The snapshot copies covered only the truncate transaction. Removing the clear and snapshots preserves untouched active keys while existing same-key controls still consume attribution."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The production change removes the per-transaction snapshots and clears only row origins, completed attribution, and the virtual-props cache at truncate."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M03"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reentrant grammar never wrote a later different key with an active edit."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct coverage gap."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The former fixed histories wrote key 1 only. The new key 2 history failed on the original production logic at the promised public cuts."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Two optimistic-visibility variants join the primary publication owner; the coverage map names the bounded shape."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M04"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A pending manual transaction's shared handler could take a later edit's deferred result."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797"},{"type":"text","value":". The prior review record documents a later-edit RED settlement assertion and a hostile shared-handler variant; the current driver binds its own deferred."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"already-fixed."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Preserve the later-edit witness and settlement law in the optimistic-history owner."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M05"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejection of a pending manual request never commits it, so the model fails it while production stays pending."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797"},{"type":"text","value":". The prior RED assertion and current guarded rejection, commit, and cleanup are recorded above."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"already-fixed."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Preserve success, direct rollback, and rejection cases; no second patch to the handler was needed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M06"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The no-cascade model comment is false for a failed same-key edit and pending manual peer."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Explicit cascading rollback and handler rejection made production reject the peer while the old model kept its overlay. The mismatch reached settlement, not a timeout."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model cancels pending same-key peers; six cases distinguish same/different keys, cascading/secondary rollback, and handler rejection."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M07"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The real SQLite test rejects an outbound promise that no handler observes if setup fails before insert."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A temporary pre-insert failure produced both the intended error and a Vitest unhandled rejection. The same injection after the guard preserved only the primary failure."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Reject outbound only once a transaction exists; this protects test failure fidelity."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M08"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model keeps active keys through truncate while production clears them, without explaining the difference."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct explanatory and implementation gap."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model's active-key rule was the independent expected behavior that exposed M01; its comment did not distinguish active from completed attribution."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Opening and local model prose now explain why untouched active keys survive and completed one-use attribution ends."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M09"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The origin contract is repeated across source, glossary, guide, and generated reference pages and had drifted."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The same detailed paragraph appeared six times. The generated reference copy also stated the broken drain rule."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" source prose is the detailed contract; the interface, glossary, and guide summarize and link to its generated reference entry. Five affected reference pages carry the regenerated text and corrected source anchors."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No original severity labels were supplied. M01 is the release-relevant product\nregression; M03, M04, M05, M06, and M08 concern oracle reach or judgment; M07\nconcerns test failure fidelity; M09 concerns contract drift. The reviewer was\ntechnically accurate on all nine items at the reviewed commit and found the\ninteraction between the prior fix and active-key ownership. The proposed M02\nrepair was both correct and smaller than the snapshot approach. The review\ndid not report execution, so its failure claims needed the probes above."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"laws-reachable-histories-and-enforcement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Laws, reachable histories, and enforcement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L6: Truncate preserves active attribution for untouched keys (M01–M03,\nM08–M09)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" contract and the existing key-and-timing\nmodel require a source write on an active local mutation's key to be local\nuntil a source operation consumes that key. A truncate ends completed one-use\nattribution, but an active mutation on a key the truncate omits is still active.\nThe old production clear contradicted this law. The old oracle lacked the\ndifferent-key suffix, so it never distinguished the clear from the model.\nThe independent model retains active keys until a source operation touches\nthem and clears completed grants at truncate. The legal fixed history has one\nactive edit on key 2, a subscriber-triggering truncate, a second truncate\nwriting key 1, then a non-truncate key 2 source transaction in the same drain.\nBoth optimistic and nonoptimistic edit visibility are tested. The existing\nlater same-key suffix controls the opposite boundary: a key written by the\ntruncate loses attribution for a later transaction. The real Collection sync\ndriver compares public rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream\nrows, outcomes, and complete publication cuts after each step. On the old\nimplementation, the nonoptimistic case failed at source publication and the\noptimistic case failed when the failed edit's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" settled: actual\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remote"},{"type":"text","value":", expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"local"},{"type":"text","value":". Those are assertion failures at the intended\ncuts. The repaired code passes both and the same-key controls. This closes the\ntwo-key, one-active-edit, two-suffix-batch core boundary tested here; it does\nnot establish every longer reentrant drain or provider bridge sequence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L7: A cascading failure cancels a pending same-key peer (M06)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Public\ntransaction settlement and the established same-key conflict rule require a\npending manual peer to reject when a failing edit cascades rollback to it.\nA different-key peer, an already persisting peer, or a secondary rollback\ndoes not acquire that outcome from this failure. The old model comment and\ntransition wrongly omitted the pending-peer cancellation, allowing false\nfailures against correct production behavior. The model now uses its own\none-Collection key equality and pending state to derive the canceled peers;\nit does not import the production conflict helper. The six fixed histories\ncross same/different keys with direct cascading rollback, secondary rollback,\nand handler rejection. They run real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutate"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollback"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":". Each step compares\nvisible rows, origin, event replicas, downstream rows, request outcomes, and\npublication cuts. The old no-cascade model failed at settlement in the\nsame-key cases; the corrected model passes all six, while the different-key\nand secondary controls distinguish overbroad cancellation. This closes the\ntwo-request pending-peer boundary tested here, not arbitrary conflict graphs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L8: A pending manual request settles by its own handler (M04–M05)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The\ncontract, grammar, core path, settlement observations, original RED results,\nshared-handler hostile control, and remaining longer-history limit are\ndocumented under CR3 above. This review rechecked that the current driver\nstill uses that path and that its focused suite remains green. M04 and M05\nwere true of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e659d413e"},{"type":"text","value":" but required no new repair after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"M07 needed a controlled failure injection, not a product model: the test\ncleanup must not create an unhandled rejection that masks its primary setup\nfailure. M09 needed direct source comparison, TypeDoc output, and working\nrelative links, not a state oracle. The five generated reference pages contain\none detailed contract in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" and summaries elsewhere; their\nsource anchors match the current declarations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"oracle-guide-audit-and-verification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle-guide audit and verification"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome for this review"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001/003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"L6–L8 state authority, finite limits, model rules, grammar, production path, and comparison cuts beside the executable owner. The canonical origin prose and coverage map were updated with the executable law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002/008/009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model retains active keys independently of production tracking and distinguishes pending from persisting. Cancellation uses model key/state, not the production helper. The model-only row action and public terms remain mapped in the opening."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004/013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The new key 2 suffix reconstructs the reported trace; same-key later-write and both visibility modes distinguish wrong boundaries. The six peer histories ablate key equality and cascade mode. The existing grammar rejects a reentrant suffix without truncate. Domains and longer-history limits are explicit above and in the coverage map."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005/006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Original truncate logic and old no-cascade model failed assertions at named public cuts. The earlier manual-handler omissions and shared-handler mutant failed at settlement. No timeout, setup error, or mere path reach is counted as a kill."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The additions are bounded fixed histories, not a new important generated property. Existing generated campaigns were not changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The real SQLite pre-insert injection separated its primary error from the unhandled rejection; the guard removed the latter. Existing cleanup aggregation still preserves primary history failures."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No new shared semantic fault between production and the independent model was identified, so a second formulation is not triggered. Same-key and different-key controls test the boundary; no new real-provider equivalence is claimed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012/014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record binds the reviewed head and RED/GREEN evidence. The core reentrant claim stays at the core Collection boundary; the private bridge still needs its callback/write sequence and a persisted receiver."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After the repair, 241 core tests passed across optimistic publication,\noutcomes, state retention, and truncate readiness, with no test type errors.\nThe SQLite real-adapter and persisted oracle run passed 869 tests with two\nexisting todo tests and no test type errors. The doc generator emitted the five\nrelevant reference pages, but also produced unrelated changes because several\npackage dependencies were unavailable; those unrelated changes were not part\nof this repair. The focused source and link checks found no stale repeated\norigin paragraph or invalid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" relative link."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final loss audit for this review: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9 raw items = 7 fixed-now + 2\nalready-fixed"}]},{"type":"text","value":". There are no refutations, accepted-design items, design\ndecisions, or agreed deferrals. The reviewed claims are accounted for and\nthe authorized bounded repairs are complete. No universal claim is made for\nlonger reentrant suffixes, larger rollback conflict graphs, other SQLite\nhosts, or the unavailable private #2071 bridge sequence; their executable\nowners and needed witnesses remain in the coverage map."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"coderabbit-review-of-ee21b1797","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460095613","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review 5460095613"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ncontained one outside-diff behavior suggestion and one inline documentation\nsuggestion. Both are accounted for here; its suggested agent prompts and local\ntool invocation are review metadata, not additional findings."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim and proposed action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence and technical verdict"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition and durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model keeps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeKeys"},{"type":"text","value":" after an empty truncate while production clears its active-key tracking; clear "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeKeys"},{"type":"text","value":" after taking the batch snapshot."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production did clear active tracking on the reviewed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ee21b1797"},{"type":"text","value":", so the observation was accurate there. Its proposed model change contradicts the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualOrigin"},{"type":"text","value":" active-key law and would make the model accept M01's product regression. After the production repair at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a109687c1"},{"type":"text","value":", that change was run as a temporary model mutant. Both new key 2 cases failed at their intended public cuts: the nonoptimistic case at complete source publication and the optimistic case at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted"},{"type":"text","value":" settlement. The mutant was removed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stale"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as a production/model mismatch after M01's repair; "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"refuted"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as a model repair. Keep the empty-truncate/different-key challenge under the optimistic-history owner."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Remove a hiring recommendation from the engineering review record."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Correct maintainability point: the durable record needs code and test evidence; the user-facing review can carry the requested reviewer assessment."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Removed the personnel recommendation from this record while retaining the technical quality assessment and all law evidence."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit accounting for this review: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2 raw items = 1 stale with a refuted\nrepair + 1 fixed-now"}]},{"type":"text","value":". CR4's historical observation and useful adversarial\nhistory remain recorded; no product law or test coverage was weakened to\naccept the proposed model change."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"merge-readiness-self-review-after-ci-passed-on-7dcebc75f","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Merge-readiness self-review after CI passed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7dcebc75f"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public origin prose said that a later same-key transaction after a\ntruncate is remote without specifying the drain boundary. The existing\nreentrant witness has a trigger and suffix truncate in separate drains. The\nstill-active mutation can attribute the suffix truncate again. A later\nsame-key transaction within the suffix drain is remote. The source comment,\ngenerated reference, oracle opening, publication comment, and coverage map\nnow state that narrower rule. This is a contract wording correction, not a\nproduction change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two temporary legal-history probes also crossed a pending and persisting\nsame-key pair with a reentrant truncate, and two active keys with that drain.\nBoth passed the core public-history comparison and test type check. The probes\nwere removed. They support those two sampled schedules but do not close the\ncoverage map's longer overlap or provider-path limits."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"coderabbit-review-of-c7d99a43f","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit review of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c7d99a43f"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460769310","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review 5460769310"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ncontained one inline documentation finding. The review body also suggested\nrunning the local CodeRabbit CLI after a fix. Both items are accounted for\nbelow."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim and proposed action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence and technical verdict"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition and durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The “after green CI” heading implied the current head had passed; change it to say CI was pending."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The preceding head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7dcebc75f"},{"type":"text","value":" had passed its 26 checks, but checks for reviewed head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c7d99a43f"},{"type":"text","value":" were still pending at review time. The unqualified heading was ambiguous."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fixed-now."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The heading now names the head whose CI had passed, preserving the historical sequence without implying current-head success."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CR7"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider running "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"coderabbit review --agent"},{"type":"text","value":" after the fix."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This was an optional workflow prompt, not a code or contract finding. The hosted review already examined "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c7d99a43f"},{"type":"text","value":"; the local CLI would add no required evidence for this wording change."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"refuted"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as an additional PR requirement. Keep the hosted review and CI status as the review evidence."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit accounting for this review: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2 raw items = 1 fixed-now + 1\nrefuted"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This documentation finding affects no product law or oracle path;\nits evidence is the recorded commit order and CI status at review time."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"pr-2078-origin-attribution-and-refused-insert-review","text":"PR #2078: origin attribution and refused insert review","level":1},{"id":"finding-ledger","text":"Finding ledger","level":2},{"id":"laws-and-enforcement","text":"Laws and enforcement","level":2},{"id":"l1-active-local-keys-and-pending-settlement-f01-f02","text":"L1. Active local keys and pending settlement (F01, F02)","level":3},{"id":"l2-truncate-attribution-ends-with-its-own-source-transaction-f03","text":"L2. Truncate attribution ends with its own source transaction (F03)","level":3},{"id":"l3-ordered-operations-retain-one-atomic-origin-f04","text":"L3. Ordered operations retain one atomic origin (F04)","level":3},{"id":"l4-successful-overlapping-mutations-preserve-one-key-grant-f08","text":"L4. Successful overlapping mutations preserve one key grant (F08)","level":3},{"id":"l5-real-sqlite-receives-source-updates-after-refusal-f05-f07","text":"L5. Real SQLite receives source updates after refusal (F05, F07)","level":3},{"id":"oracle-guide-check","text":"Oracle-guide check","level":2},{"id":"verification-and-limits","text":"Verification and limits","level":2},{"id":"post-audit-ci-and-review-follow-ups","text":"Post-audit CI and review follow-ups","level":2},{"id":"cr3-law-reach-and-calibration","text":"CR3 law, reach, and calibration","level":3},{"id":"subsequent-nine-finding-review-of-e659d413e","text":"Subsequent nine-finding review of e659d413e","level":2},{"id":"laws-reachable-histories-and-enforcement","text":"Laws, reachable histories, and enforcement","level":3},{"id":"oracle-guide-audit-and-verification","text":"Oracle-guide audit and verification","level":3},{"id":"coderabbit-review-of-ee21b1797","text":"CodeRabbit review of ee21b1797","level":3},{"id":"merge-readiness-self-review-after-ci-passed-on-7dcebc75f","text":"Merge-readiness self-review after CI passed on 7dcebc75f","level":3},{"id":"coderabbit-review-of-c7d99a43f","text":"CodeRabbit review of c7d99a43f","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# PR #2078: origin attribution and refused insert review\n\nReview target: `a4e6acd33dbb41ca220541ef21414efb3fbbb5ce` against\n`2c98b4992c412820f8476325ee6941db5ca9ac0f`. The eight external findings\nwere supplied in source order. The repair and executable oracle revision is\n`43608b4f94a845de3e8d8be2373c42b0ab65efb7`. This record follows that\ncommit; it does not change its executable code. The private issue #2071 bridge\nhistory remains unavailable and is not treated as refuted.\n\n## Finding ledger\n\n| ID | Reviewed claim | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable destination |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F01 | `$origin` text omits pending transactions; a same-key source row can stay local after pending rollback, without truncate. | True. A manual pending transaction does not hold source commits. Four controlled cells cross optimistic visibility and fulfillment/rollback, and compare origin at source publication and settlement. | **fixed-now**: API comments, glossary, guide, both reference pages, and the primary optimistic-history model. |\n| F02 | `pendingLocalChanges` says persisting, but includes pending. | True by the `overlayActiveTransactions()` state predicate and F01's public witness. | **fixed-now**: production comment names pending and persisting. |\n| F03 | A truncate's retained origin leaks to a later same-key transaction in the same drain. | True product bug. The new reentrant history was RED on the reviewed production code: at failed-mutation settlement, the model expected `remote` and the public row was `local`. A one-transaction control passed. | **fixed-now**: scope retained snapshots to each sync transaction in `state.ts`; the same oracle is GREEN. |\n| F04 | The source-batch model cannot express delete then reinsert within one transaction, and its opening omits that limit. | True on the reviewed head. The grammar now preserves mixed source operation order, and the opening states the default and ordered lanes. | **fixed-now**: primary model/driver and atomic versus later-transaction witnesses. |\n| F05 | The real SQLite test claims an update but writes an insert. | True. The original continuation had insert, delete, insert. It now writes a real `update` and checks adapter forwarding, live/base/durable rows, and reopen. | **fixed-now**: real adapter receiving witness and accurate comment. |\n| F06 | `cleanupRealWitness` duplicates `cleanupPersistedOracle`. | Substantially true shared loop, though the latter also enforces a 250 ms limit per action. | **fixed-now**: one `test-cleanup.ts` helper; the persisted owner keeps its timeout wrapper. |\n| F07 | The first Collection is cleaned twice; a failure may remove the directory before resources settle. | The double call was true. The claimed open SQLite handle was not established: this witness uses a CLI driver with one process per operation. The old finalizer still attempted directory removal after cleanup failure. | **fixed-now**: track both Collections' successful cleanup and remove the directory only after both release; retain the primary failure and cleanup diagnostics. |\n| F08 | The guide's timing rules leave overlapping same-key mutations unspecified while the oracle stays green. | The coverage gap is true. The guide already qualified its first-transaction rule to one mutation without truncate, so it did not promise every overlap. Sixteen bounded histories now cross two active same-key mutations, one/two queued source transactions, both settlement orders, and all success/failure pairs. | **fixed-now** for that bounded gap: guide, model, publication driver, and coverage map. Broader overlap schedules remain explicitly outside the closure claim. |\n\nThe original review had eight items. All eight received an in-PR action. F07's\nopen-handle mechanism and F08's claim of an unqualified guide promise were\noverstated; those qualifications do not erase their useful findings.\n\n## Laws and enforcement\n\n### L1. Active local keys and pending settlement (F01, F02)\n\nAuthority: the key-and-timing `$origin` contract in `virtual-props.ts` and the\nglossary. A pending manual local transaction is active before its mutation\nfunction starts. A same-key source commit applies immediately, may receive\nlocal attribution, and keeps that origin after the manual transaction rolls\nback. The source identity is unknown. The old model had only `persisting`,\n`completed`, and `failed`, so its grammar could not reach the reported cut.\n\nThe model now distinguishes `pending` from `persisting`: the same source commit\napplies immediately in the former state and queues in the latter. The driver\nuses public `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })`, `mutate`, sync writes,\n`commit` or `rollback`, and Collection reads. Four cells cross optimistic\nvisibility and success/failure. After each step, the shared driver compares\npublic rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request\noutcomes, and complete publication cuts. A temporary production mutant that\nrequired `hasPersistingTransaction()` before consulting `pendingLocalChanges`\nreached the source and settlement cuts and failed all four cells. It was\nrestored before the executable commit. This establishes the bounded manual\none-key path, not every pending and persisting overlap.\n\n### L2. Truncate attribution ends with its own source transaction (F03)\n\nAuthority: the existing per-atomic-transaction attribution contract and the\ntruncate comment in `state.ts`. A subscriber can submit a truncate and a later\nsame-key source transaction while core drains an earlier truncate. Those\naccepted suffix transactions apply in source order during the next drain.\nAttribution retained for the truncate must not color the later transaction.\n\nThe old driver could only commit one source batch at a time, so a truncate\ndrained before it could queue a successor in the same drain. The new reentrant\nstep commits both from a real Collection subscriber. The independent model\nqueues the suffix and snapshots attribution for each batch. The one-batch\ncontrol expects local attribution; the two-batch history expects remote for\nthe later row. On the original production branch, the second history failed\nat the public row observed when `isPersisted` rejected: expected `remote`, got\n`local`. Moving the retained sets inside the transaction loop made both\nhistories pass. The driver also checks event replicas and downstream state.\nCoverage is one active mutation, two truncates, and zero or one later same-key\ntransaction; arbitrary reentrant suffixes are not claimed.\n\n### L3. Ordered operations retain one atomic origin (F04)\n\nAuthority: a sync transaction is the `begin()`/`commit()` atomic batch, and\nthe existing first-transaction origin law. The model's `row` action is a\ngrammar abstraction over source insert/update; the driver selects the source\nwrite kind from its own key membership. The model does not import that driver\nclassifier. Ordered operations now represent delete then reinsert in one\nbatch. The model snapshots origin at batch start, consumes it for later\ntransactions, and derives expected rows independently of production caches.\n\nThe fixed witness reaches the real sync path with delete then reinsert of an\nexisting key. A neighboring history adds a later same-key transaction. Public\norigin at successful settlement is local in the atomic-only case and remote\nafter the later transaction. A temporary production mutant that omitted a\ndelete from the batch-local attribution set failed the atomic-only witness at\nsettlement, while the later-transaction control passed. The ordered grammar\nrejects mixed `operations` with `rows`/`deletes` and more than one copy. Other\nmixed operation sequences are representable but not exhaustively sampled.\n\n### L4. Successful overlapping mutations preserve one key grant (F08)\n\nAuthority: queued source attribution is by key and timing, not by source\nauthor or one grant per optimistic transaction. If two same-key mutations are\nboth persisting before a source transaction queues, a successful mutation\nretains one attribution for the key even if its sibling fails. Two failures\nretain none. The first same-key source transaction consumes that grant; a\nsecond source transaction is remote without another local owner.\n\nThe model already represented multiple persisting transactions but had no\ndistinguishing overlap campaign. Sixteen bounded histories now cross the two\noutcomes, settlement order, and one/two source transactions. The core driver\ncompares public origin at each settlement, full event cuts, and downstream\nrows. A temporary production mutant clearing `pendingLocalOrigins` whenever a\nfailed sibling remained failed four one-success/one-source cells at the\nsettlement read, while two-failure controls passed. The tested histories do\nnot include pending plus persisting overlap, multiple keys, truncate during\noverlap, or all possible handler schedules. The coverage map retains those\nlimits; no universal overlap proof is claimed.\n\n### L5. Real SQLite receives source updates after refusal (F05, F07)\n\nAuthority: accepted source transactions must reach the Collection and its\ndurable persisted replica; a rejected local insert contributes no durable row.\nThe real SQLite witness holds outbound rejection, accepts a same-key delete,\nthen follows one of two histories. The continuation now inserts, deletes,\nreinserts, and updates through `SyncConfig.write`. It checks live and exposed\nbase rows, `applyCommittedTx`'s update mutation, durable `loadSubset`, and a\nfresh Collection/adapter/driver reopened over the same file. A temporary\nadapter mutant dropped only the final update before durable application. It\nreached the new update cut and failed the durable-row comparison: SQLite kept\n`later source row` instead of `updated source row`. The original broad mutant\nalso dropped an earlier source turn and failed too early; that result is not\ncounted as update calibration. The receiving witness uses a SQLite CLI driver\nand a same-process reopen; other hosts and a process restart remain outside.\n\n## Oracle-guide check\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 authority and limits | L1–L5 name the contract and finite path limits. The private bridge remains unknown. |\n| ORC-002 independent judgment | The model derives rows and origin from source actions and key ownership, without production caches or classifiers. The shared `sourceOperations` function only expands grammar order. |\n| ORC-003 responsibilities | Opening prose states laws and limits; `HistoryModel`, `OptimisticStep`, the real Collection driver, and `check()` keep model, grammar, path, and refinement distinct. |\n| ORC-004 grammar controls | Pending versus persisting, one versus two queued source transactions, batch versus transaction boundary, and both settlement orders have reconstructing witnesses. Bounded domains are above. The driver rejects an ordered batch mixed with legacy rows/deletes or multiple copies, and a reentrant trigger without truncate. Other legal schedules remain listed. |\n| ORC-005 path and observation | Public Collection writes, sync callbacks, subscription replicas, downstream rows, receipts, and settlement-time reads reach the named cuts. The real adapter path checks durable and reopened rows. |\n| ORC-006 calibration | The original production code and four targeted hostile edits fail assertions at the intended public cuts. The too-broad SQLite edit failed early and is excluded from the update claim. No timeout or setup error is counted as a kill. |\n| ORC-007 fixed/random parity | Not triggered by the new bounded enumerations and fixed histories. Existing generated campaigns in the owner retain their prior configuration. |\n| ORC-008 model minimality | Pending differs from persisting because sync applies now versus waits. Source operation order and a combined reentrant drain change legal next observations. |\n| ORC-009 vocabulary | `pending`, `persisting`, sync transaction, publication, and settlement match the glossary. The model-only `row` action combines source insert/update as stated in the opening. |\n| ORC-010 failure fidelity | The core owner keeps its primary failure through cleanup. The SQLite owners now share cleanup aggregation; the persisted owner still bounds each stage, and the real witness releases Collections before directory removal. |\n| ORC-011 second formulation | Atomic delete/reinsert versus a later transaction challenges batch-boundary semantics. Causal source authorship cannot be independently decided without a source identity signal; no such guarantee is claimed. |\n| ORC-012 review evidence | This record binds original head, executable revision, RED/GREEN, mutants, finite closure, and remaining cells. |\n| ORC-013 distinguishing boundary | One versus two source transactions, one versus two reentrant suffix batches, pending versus persisting, and one/two overlapping outcomes distinguish plausible wrong timing rules at public cuts. |\n| ORC-014 controlled handoff | The real SQLite receiver tests the controlled refused-insert and source-update premise. The core reentrant history is a core claim, not claimed as real-provider coverage. |\n\n## Verification and limits\n\nOn the executable revision, 166 core optimistic history and state-retention\ntests passed; the commit-hook rerun passed 61 core publication and outcome tests.\nThe relevant persisted and real SQLite run passed 18 tests, with unrelated\npersisted cases filtered. Vitest's test type checks passed. Prettier and the\nchanged-file ESLint run had no errors; ESLint reported pre-existing warnings\nin the large persisted oracle, and one new shadow warning was repaired before\ncommit. Git's commit hook ran ESLint fixes; the two focused suites were rerun\nafter the commit. The production diff in `state.ts` is seven added and six\nremoved lines, including the corrected comment.\n\nThe issue #2071 bridge sequence is still unavailable. This review does not\nclaim to close its private path, every overlap schedule, every mixed source\nbatch, another SQLite host, or process restart. The optimistic-history owner\ncan represent mixed source order and bounded reentry; a bridge-specific\ncounterexample needs the callback/write sequence and a persisted-wrapper\nreceiving witness. The coverage map names those limits. No authorized in-scope\nrepair from these eight findings is left open.\n\nFinal accounting: **8 raw items = 8 fixed-now**. F07 and F08 include the\ntechnical qualifications recorded above; neither was dropped from the ledger.\n\n## Post-audit CI and review follow-ups\n\nThe full `db-2` CI shard on `00fcf0f` passed 5,901 runtime tests but reported a\ntest type error at `optimistic-history-oracle.ts:987`: the new manual\n`Transaction<HistoryRow>` was missing from the driver's transaction union.\nCommit `9cb0e9998ba7fc4f8bb67f2ddc6c45e58717c24b` adds that type. The\npackage test TypeScript check and 42 origin-publication tests, including their\nVitest type check, pass locally. This change does not alter the model or\nproduction behavior.\n\nCodeRabbit then reviewed `00fcf0f` and posted two actionable documentation\ncomments. Both were correct. The earlier issue #2071 review record used present\ntense for the source-batch grammar's former delete/reinsert limit; its affected\nstatements now name the historical commit and point to the ordered-batch witness\nadded in this PR. The generated `VirtualRowProps` and `VirtualOrigin` reference\npages linked to old source line numbers; all seven declaration links now match\n`virtual-props.ts`. These follow-ups do not change the eight-item accounting\nabove. All reported CI checks passed on `e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3`.\n\nCodeRabbit's next review, [5459093045](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5459093045),\nchecked that head and found an oracle harness defect. Its three findings across\nboth reviews are accounted for separately from the user's eight findings:\n\n| ID | Claim | Verdict and evidence | PR action and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR1 | The earlier #2071 review record still claimed that delete then reinsert was outside the source-batch grammar. | Correct on `00fcf0f`; the ordered grammar and coverage-map witness existed by that commit. | **fixed-now** in `e659d413e`; historical wording now identifies the earlier limit and later witness. |\n| CR2 | Six `VirtualRowProps` anchors and one `VirtualOrigin` anchor pointed into source comments. | Correct on `00fcf0f`; all seven current anchors match the declarations at lines 74, 85, 104, 133, 141, 149, and 36. | **fixed-now** in `e659d413e`; generated reference links were corrected. |\n| CR3 | A rejected pending manual transaction never starts its handler, so its `isPersisted` promise and oracle cleanup can remain pending. Commit the transaction after rejecting its handler promise. | Correct on `e659d413e`. Two source-row histories and one later-edit history failed at the settlement row assertion after the test first made cleanup safe. The proposed single-line fix alone leaves a later-edit history pending because the shared `starting` variable points to the later request. | **fixed-now** in this follow-up: commit the rejected manual request, bind each manual handler to its own deferred result, and release any still-active transaction during cleanup. The primary oracle and coverage map retain the law and limits. |\n\nThe reviewer's three findings were accurate and focused. The proposed fix for\nCR3 identified the missing commit but did not account for a later edit replacing\nthe shared deferred promise. The reviews distinguished documentation defects\nfrom a test-integrity defect and had little noise. Adjacent interleavings\nstill need checking before accepting the proposed patch verbatim.\n\n### CR3 law, reach, and calibration\n\nA pending manual optimistic transaction starts its mutation function only when\ncommitted. Handler rejection must roll it back, reject `isPersisted` with the\nsame reason, and expose the applied source row after dropping any optimistic\noverlay. This follows from the optimistic transaction and settlement entries in\nthe glossary and from `Transaction.commit()`/`rollback()`. The production path\nis `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })` → `mutate()` → `commit()` → the\ncontrolled handler promise → `isPersisted`. The model already represented\npending, persisting, and failed transactions. Its grammar could select a\nrejection but the pending fixed witnesses selected only success or direct\nrollback. The driver did not commit on rejection, and cleanup tried rollback\nonly when the expected result was still pending.\n\nThe expanded fixed matrix crosses optimistic visibility with success, direct\nrollback, and handler rejection after a same-key source insert. A second pair\nstarts a later disjoint edit before the manual request succeeds or rejects.\nAfter each step, the driver checks public rows, origin and pending-write\nmetadata, subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request outcome and reason,\nand publication cuts. At settlement it also checks the rows captured when\n`isPersisted` settles. The source-row and later-edit rejection cases failed at\nthat settlement assertion on the reviewed harness. They were assertion\nfailures, not Vitest timeouts; cleanup preserved the primary mismatch and\nreleased the pending transaction. With the commit fix in place, temporarily\nrestoring the shared handler made both later-edit cases fail at the same\nassertion. Binding the manual handler to its own deferred result makes all\neight targeted cases pass. This calibrates both the original omission and the\nplausible one-line repair that misses an adjacent legal history.\n\nThis establishes the bounded law for the named manual histories through the\ncore Collection path and public settlement, row, and publication checkpoints.\nIt does not prove longer manual interleavings, same-key cascades, or a persisted\nadapter path. The coverage map keeps those limits under the optimistic history\nowner. CR3 changes the oracle driver and its fixed histories, not the product\nimplementation or model state.\n\n| Guide requirement | CR3 evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001/003 | The oracle's opening states the manual settlement law and limits; the model, grammar, driver, and per-step comparison remain distinct. |\n| ORC-002 | The reference transition remains independent of transaction implementation; no expected state was copied from the new driver. |\n| ORC-004/007 | The new cases are bounded fixed histories; generated grammar and fixed/random campaigns did not change. |\n| ORC-005/006 | Real core transactions reach `isPersisted` and public rows. Original and shared-handler variants fail at the intended settlement assertion; the repaired driver passes. |\n| ORC-008/009 | Model state did not change. `pending`, `persisting`, `failed`, and settlement retain glossary meanings. |\n| ORC-010 | Cleanup rolls back a still-active transaction regardless of expected outcome; the original assertion remains the aggregate cause, with secondary diagnostics retained. The deferred rejection has a handler before a manual commit starts. |\n| ORC-011 | No plausible shared semantic fault between production and model was identified; this review concerned the production driver's ability to reach the modeled outcome. |\n| ORC-012/013 | This record binds the reviewed head, RED/GREEN cuts, hostile variant, neighboring success/rollback cases, and remaining limits. |\n| ORC-014 | The claim is limited to the core Collection path and makes no real-provider handoff claim. |\n\nFinal CodeRabbit accounting: **3 raw findings = 3 fixed-now**. There are no\ndeferred or design-decision items. The original user's eight-item audit remains\n**8 raw = 8 fixed-now**; the unknown private #2071 bridge sequence remains an\nevidence limit, not a CodeRabbit item.\n\n## Subsequent nine-finding review of `e659d413e`\n\nThe external medium-effort review examined `e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3`\nby reading code; it reported no executed checks. Evaluation began on\n`ee21b1797eb7cfc9dfa18a05637477a10de67828`, which had already repaired\nthe manual-transaction defects in M04 and M05. The nine numbered findings are\nall the review's claims; its closing remarks add context, not a tenth item.\n\n| ID | Review claim and proposed repair | Technical verdict and evidence | PR disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| M01 | A truncate on key 1 clears active attribution for untouched key 2; a later key 2 source transaction in the same drain becomes remote. | **Correct, high severity.** A real subscriber committed a truncate suffix and later key 2 write while an edit on key 2 remained active. The public row was remote where the independent model required local. Both publication and settlement variants failed on the reviewed production logic. | **fixed-now.** Preserve active keys across truncate; the primary optimistic-history oracle owns the origin law. |\n| M02 | Clearing `pendingLocalChanges` on truncate caused M01; keep it and remove the temporary origin snapshots. | **Correct.** The snapshot copies covered only the truncate transaction. Removing the clear and snapshots preserves untouched active keys while existing same-key controls still consume attribution. | **fixed-now.** The production change removes the per-transaction snapshots and clears only row origins, completed attribution, and the virtual-props cache at truncate. |\n| M03 | The reentrant grammar never wrote a later different key with an active edit. | **Correct coverage gap.** The former fixed histories wrote key 1 only. The new key 2 history failed on the original production logic at the promised public cuts. | **fixed-now.** Two optimistic-visibility variants join the primary publication owner; the coverage map names the bounded shape. |\n| M04 | A pending manual transaction's shared handler could take a later edit's deferred result. | **Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed** in `ee21b1797`. The prior review record documents a later-edit RED settlement assertion and a hostile shared-handler variant; the current driver binds its own deferred. | **already-fixed.** Preserve the later-edit witness and settlement law in the optimistic-history owner. |\n| M05 | Rejection of a pending manual request never commits it, so the model fails it while production stays pending. | **Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed** in `ee21b1797`. The prior RED assertion and current guarded rejection, commit, and cleanup are recorded above. | **already-fixed.** Preserve success, direct rollback, and rejection cases; no second patch to the handler was needed. |\n| M06 | The no-cascade model comment is false for a failed same-key edit and pending manual peer. | **Correct.** Explicit cascading rollback and handler rejection made production reject the peer while the old model kept its overlay. The mismatch reached settlement, not a timeout. | **fixed-now.** The model cancels pending same-key peers; six cases distinguish same/different keys, cascading/secondary rollback, and handler rejection. |\n| M07 | The real SQLite test rejects an outbound promise that no handler observes if setup fails before insert. | **Correct.** A temporary pre-insert failure produced both the intended error and a Vitest unhandled rejection. The same injection after the guard preserved only the primary failure. | **fixed-now.** Reject outbound only once a transaction exists; this protects test failure fidelity. |\n| M08 | The model keeps active keys through truncate while production clears them, without explaining the difference. | **Correct explanatory and implementation gap.** The model's active-key rule was the independent expected behavior that exposed M01; its comment did not distinguish active from completed attribution. | **fixed-now.** Opening and local model prose now explain why untouched active keys survive and completed one-use attribution ends. |\n| M09 | The origin contract is repeated across source, glossary, guide, and generated reference pages and had drifted. | **Correct.** The same detailed paragraph appeared six times. The generated reference copy also stated the broken drain rule. | **fixed-now.** `VirtualOrigin` source prose is the detailed contract; the interface, glossary, and guide summarize and link to its generated reference entry. Five affected reference pages carry the regenerated text and corrected source anchors. |\n\nNo original severity labels were supplied. M01 is the release-relevant product\nregression; M03, M04, M05, M06, and M08 concern oracle reach or judgment; M07\nconcerns test failure fidelity; M09 concerns contract drift. The reviewer was\ntechnically accurate on all nine items at the reviewed commit and found the\ninteraction between the prior fix and active-key ownership. The proposed M02\nrepair was both correct and smaller than the snapshot approach. The review\ndid not report execution, so its failure claims needed the probes above.\n\n### Laws, reachable histories, and enforcement\n\n**L6: Truncate preserves active attribution for untouched keys (M01–M03,\nM08–M09).** The public `VirtualOrigin` contract and the existing key-and-timing\nmodel require a source write on an active local mutation's key to be local\nuntil a source operation consumes that key. A truncate ends completed one-use\nattribution, but an active mutation on a key the truncate omits is still active.\nThe old production clear contradicted this law. The old oracle lacked the\ndifferent-key suffix, so it never distinguished the clear from the model.\nThe independent model retains active keys until a source operation touches\nthem and clears completed grants at truncate. The legal fixed history has one\nactive edit on key 2, a subscriber-triggering truncate, a second truncate\nwriting key 1, then a non-truncate key 2 source transaction in the same drain.\nBoth optimistic and nonoptimistic edit visibility are tested. The existing\nlater same-key suffix controls the opposite boundary: a key written by the\ntruncate loses attribution for a later transaction. The real Collection sync\ndriver compares public rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream\nrows, outcomes, and complete publication cuts after each step. On the old\nimplementation, the nonoptimistic case failed at source publication and the\noptimistic case failed when the failed edit's `isPersisted` settled: actual\n`remote`, expected `local`. Those are assertion failures at the intended\ncuts. The repaired code passes both and the same-key controls. This closes the\ntwo-key, one-active-edit, two-suffix-batch core boundary tested here; it does\nnot establish every longer reentrant drain or provider bridge sequence.\n\n**L7: A cascading failure cancels a pending same-key peer (M06).** Public\ntransaction settlement and the established same-key conflict rule require a\npending manual peer to reject when a failing edit cascades rollback to it.\nA different-key peer, an already persisting peer, or a secondary rollback\ndoes not acquire that outcome from this failure. The old model comment and\ntransition wrongly omitted the pending-peer cancellation, allowing false\nfailures against correct production behavior. The model now uses its own\none-Collection key equality and pending state to derive the canceled peers;\nit does not import the production conflict helper. The six fixed histories\ncross same/different keys with direct cascading rollback, secondary rollback,\nand handler rejection. They run real `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })`,\n`mutate`, `commit` or `rollback`, and `isPersisted`. Each step compares\nvisible rows, origin, event replicas, downstream rows, request outcomes, and\npublication cuts. The old no-cascade model failed at settlement in the\nsame-key cases; the corrected model passes all six, while the different-key\nand secondary controls distinguish overbroad cancellation. This closes the\ntwo-request pending-peer boundary tested here, not arbitrary conflict graphs.\n\n**L8: A pending manual request settles by its own handler (M04–M05).** The\ncontract, grammar, core path, settlement observations, original RED results,\nshared-handler hostile control, and remaining longer-history limit are\ndocumented under CR3 above. This review rechecked that the current driver\nstill uses that path and that its focused suite remains green. M04 and M05\nwere true of `e659d413e` but required no new repair after `ee21b1797`.\n\nM07 needed a controlled failure injection, not a product model: the test\ncleanup must not create an unhandled rejection that masks its primary setup\nfailure. M09 needed direct source comparison, TypeDoc output, and working\nrelative links, not a state oracle. The five generated reference pages contain\none detailed contract in `VirtualOrigin` and summaries elsewhere; their\nsource anchors match the current declarations.\n\n### Oracle-guide audit and verification\n\n| Requirement | Outcome for this review |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001/003 | L6–L8 state authority, finite limits, model rules, grammar, production path, and comparison cuts beside the executable owner. The canonical origin prose and coverage map were updated with the executable law. |\n| ORC-002/008/009 | The model retains active keys independently of production tracking and distinguishes pending from persisting. Cancellation uses model key/state, not the production helper. The model-only row action and public terms remain mapped in the opening. |\n| ORC-004/013 | The new key 2 suffix reconstructs the reported trace; same-key later-write and both visibility modes distinguish wrong boundaries. The six peer histories ablate key equality and cascade mode. The existing grammar rejects a reentrant suffix without truncate. Domains and longer-history limits are explicit above and in the coverage map. |\n| ORC-005/006 | Original truncate logic and old no-cascade model failed assertions at named public cuts. The earlier manual-handler omissions and shared-handler mutant failed at settlement. No timeout, setup error, or mere path reach is counted as a kill. |\n| ORC-007 | The additions are bounded fixed histories, not a new important generated property. Existing generated campaigns were not changed. |\n| ORC-010 | The real SQLite pre-insert injection separated its primary error from the unhandled rejection; the guard removed the latter. Existing cleanup aggregation still preserves primary history failures. |\n| ORC-011 | No new shared semantic fault between production and the independent model was identified, so a second formulation is not triggered. Same-key and different-key controls test the boundary; no new real-provider equivalence is claimed. |\n| ORC-012/014 | This record binds the reviewed head and RED/GREEN evidence. The core reentrant claim stays at the core Collection boundary; the private bridge still needs its callback/write sequence and a persisted receiver. |\n\nAfter the repair, 241 core tests passed across optimistic publication,\noutcomes, state retention, and truncate readiness, with no test type errors.\nThe SQLite real-adapter and persisted oracle run passed 869 tests with two\nexisting todo tests and no test type errors. The doc generator emitted the five\nrelevant reference pages, but also produced unrelated changes because several\npackage dependencies were unavailable; those unrelated changes were not part\nof this repair. The focused source and link checks found no stale repeated\norigin paragraph or invalid `VirtualOrigin` relative link.\n\nFinal loss audit for this review: **9 raw items = 7 fixed-now + 2\nalready-fixed**. There are no refutations, accepted-design items, design\ndecisions, or agreed deferrals. The reviewed claims are accounted for and\nthe authorized bounded repairs are complete. No universal claim is made for\nlonger reentrant suffixes, larger rollback conflict graphs, other SQLite\nhosts, or the unavailable private #2071 bridge sequence; their executable\nowners and needed witnesses remain in the coverage map.\n\n### CodeRabbit review of `ee21b1797`\n\n[Review 5460095613](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460095613)\ncontained one outside-diff behavior suggestion and one inline documentation\nsuggestion. Both are accounted for here; its suggested agent prompts and local\ntool invocation are review metadata, not additional findings.\n\n| ID | Claim and proposed action | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR4 | The model keeps `activeKeys` after an empty truncate while production clears its active-key tracking; clear `activeKeys` after taking the batch snapshot. | Production did clear active tracking on the reviewed `ee21b1797`, so the observation was accurate there. Its proposed model change contradicts the `VirtualOrigin` active-key law and would make the model accept M01's product regression. After the production repair at `a109687c1`, that change was run as a temporary model mutant. Both new key 2 cases failed at their intended public cuts: the nonoptimistic case at complete source publication and the optimistic case at `isPersisted` settlement. The mutant was removed. | **stale** as a production/model mismatch after M01's repair; **refuted** as a model repair. Keep the empty-truncate/different-key challenge under the optimistic-history owner. |\n| CR5 | Remove a hiring recommendation from the engineering review record. | Correct maintainability point: the durable record needs code and test evidence; the user-facing review can carry the requested reviewer assessment. | **fixed-now.** Removed the personnel recommendation from this record while retaining the technical quality assessment and all law evidence. |\n\nCodeRabbit accounting for this review: **2 raw items = 1 stale with a refuted\nrepair + 1 fixed-now**. CR4's historical observation and useful adversarial\nhistory remain recorded; no product law or test coverage was weakened to\naccept the proposed model change.\n\n### Merge-readiness self-review after CI passed on `7dcebc75f`\n\nThe public origin prose said that a later same-key transaction after a\ntruncate is remote without specifying the drain boundary. The existing\nreentrant witness has a trigger and suffix truncate in separate drains. The\nstill-active mutation can attribute the suffix truncate again. A later\nsame-key transaction within the suffix drain is remote. The source comment,\ngenerated reference, oracle opening, publication comment, and coverage map\nnow state that narrower rule. This is a contract wording correction, not a\nproduction change.\n\nTwo temporary legal-history probes also crossed a pending and persisting\nsame-key pair with a reentrant truncate, and two active keys with that drain.\nBoth passed the core public-history comparison and test type check. The probes\nwere removed. They support those two sampled schedules but do not close the\ncoverage map's longer overlap or provider-path limits.\n\n### CodeRabbit review of `c7d99a43f`\n\n[Review 5460769310](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460769310)\ncontained one inline documentation finding. The review body also suggested\nrunning the local CodeRabbit CLI after a fix. Both items are accounted for\nbelow.\n\n| ID | Claim and proposed action | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR6 | The “after green CI” heading implied the current head had passed; change it to say CI was pending. | The preceding head `7dcebc75f` had passed its 26 checks, but checks for reviewed head `c7d99a43f` were still pending at review time. The unqualified heading was ambiguous. | **fixed-now.** The heading now names the head whose CI had passed, preserving the historical sequence without implying current-head success. |\n| CR7 | Consider running `coderabbit review --agent` after the fix. | This was an optional workflow prompt, not a code or contract finding. The hosted review already examined `c7d99a43f`; the local CLI would add no required evidence for this wording change. | **refuted** as an additional PR requirement. Keep the hosted review and CI status as the review evidence. |\n\nCodeRabbit accounting for this review: **2 raw items = 1 fixed-now + 1\nrefuted**. This documentation finding affects no product law or oracle path;\nits evidence is the recorded commit order and CI status at review time.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# PR #2078: origin attribution and refused insert review\n\nReview target: `a4e6acd33dbb41ca220541ef21414efb3fbbb5ce` against\n`2c98b4992c412820f8476325ee6941db5ca9ac0f`. The eight external findings\nwere supplied in source order. The repair and executable oracle revision is\n`43608b4f94a845de3e8d8be2373c42b0ab65efb7`. This record follows that\ncommit; it does not change its executable code. The private issue #2071 bridge\nhistory remains unavailable and is not treated as refuted.\n\n## Finding ledger\n\n| ID | Reviewed claim | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable destination |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F01 | `$origin` text omits pending transactions; a same-key source row can stay local after pending rollback, without truncate. | True. A manual pending transaction does not hold source commits. Four controlled cells cross optimistic visibility and fulfillment/rollback, and compare origin at source publication and settlement. | **fixed-now**: API comments, glossary, guide, both reference pages, and the primary optimistic-history model. |\n| F02 | `pendingLocalChanges` says persisting, but includes pending. | True by the `overlayActiveTransactions()` state predicate and F01's public witness. | **fixed-now**: production comment names pending and persisting. |\n| F03 | A truncate's retained origin leaks to a later same-key transaction in the same drain. | True product bug. The new reentrant history was RED on the reviewed production code: at failed-mutation settlement, the model expected `remote` and the public row was `local`. A one-transaction control passed. | **fixed-now**: scope retained snapshots to each sync transaction in `state.ts`; the same oracle is GREEN. |\n| F04 | The source-batch model cannot express delete then reinsert within one transaction, and its opening omits that limit. | True on the reviewed head. The grammar now preserves mixed source operation order, and the opening states the default and ordered lanes. | **fixed-now**: primary model/driver and atomic versus later-transaction witnesses. |\n| F05 | The real SQLite test claims an update but writes an insert. | True. The original continuation had insert, delete, insert. It now writes a real `update` and checks adapter forwarding, live/base/durable rows, and reopen. | **fixed-now**: real adapter receiving witness and accurate comment. |\n| F06 | `cleanupRealWitness` duplicates `cleanupPersistedOracle`. | Substantially true shared loop, though the latter also enforces a 250 ms limit per action. | **fixed-now**: one `test-cleanup.ts` helper; the persisted owner keeps its timeout wrapper. |\n| F07 | The first Collection is cleaned twice; a failure may remove the directory before resources settle. | The double call was true. The claimed open SQLite handle was not established: this witness uses a CLI driver with one process per operation. The old finalizer still attempted directory removal after cleanup failure. | **fixed-now**: track both Collections' successful cleanup and remove the directory only after both release; retain the primary failure and cleanup diagnostics. |\n| F08 | The guide's timing rules leave overlapping same-key mutations unspecified while the oracle stays green. | The coverage gap is true. The guide already qualified its first-transaction rule to one mutation without truncate, so it did not promise every overlap. Sixteen bounded histories now cross two active same-key mutations, one/two queued source transactions, both settlement orders, and all success/failure pairs. | **fixed-now** for that bounded gap: guide, model, publication driver, and coverage map. Broader overlap schedules remain explicitly outside the closure claim. |\n\nThe original review had eight items. All eight received an in-PR action. F07's\nopen-handle mechanism and F08's claim of an unqualified guide promise were\noverstated; those qualifications do not erase their useful findings.\n\n## Laws and enforcement\n\n### L1. Active local keys and pending settlement (F01, F02)\n\nAuthority: the key-and-timing `$origin` contract in `virtual-props.ts` and the\nglossary. A pending manual local transaction is active before its mutation\nfunction starts. A same-key source commit applies immediately, may receive\nlocal attribution, and keeps that origin after the manual transaction rolls\nback. The source identity is unknown. The old model had only `persisting`,\n`completed`, and `failed`, so its grammar could not reach the reported cut.\n\nThe model now distinguishes `pending` from `persisting`: the same source commit\napplies immediately in the former state and queues in the latter. The driver\nuses public `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })`, `mutate`, sync writes,\n`commit` or `rollback`, and Collection reads. Four cells cross optimistic\nvisibility and success/failure. After each step, the shared driver compares\npublic rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request\noutcomes, and complete publication cuts. A temporary production mutant that\nrequired `hasPersistingTransaction()` before consulting `pendingLocalChanges`\nreached the source and settlement cuts and failed all four cells. It was\nrestored before the executable commit. This establishes the bounded manual\none-key path, not every pending and persisting overlap.\n\n### L2. Truncate attribution ends with its own source transaction (F03)\n\nAuthority: the existing per-atomic-transaction attribution contract and the\ntruncate comment in `state.ts`. A subscriber can submit a truncate and a later\nsame-key source transaction while core drains an earlier truncate. Those\naccepted suffix transactions apply in source order during the next drain.\nAttribution retained for the truncate must not color the later transaction.\n\nThe old driver could only commit one source batch at a time, so a truncate\ndrained before it could queue a successor in the same drain. The new reentrant\nstep commits both from a real Collection subscriber. The independent model\nqueues the suffix and snapshots attribution for each batch. The one-batch\ncontrol expects local attribution; the two-batch history expects remote for\nthe later row. On the original production branch, the second history failed\nat the public row observed when `isPersisted` rejected: expected `remote`, got\n`local`. Moving the retained sets inside the transaction loop made both\nhistories pass. The driver also checks event replicas and downstream state.\nCoverage is one active mutation, two truncates, and zero or one later same-key\ntransaction; arbitrary reentrant suffixes are not claimed.\n\n### L3. Ordered operations retain one atomic origin (F04)\n\nAuthority: a sync transaction is the `begin()`/`commit()` atomic batch, and\nthe existing first-transaction origin law. The model's `row` action is a\ngrammar abstraction over source insert/update; the driver selects the source\nwrite kind from its own key membership. The model does not import that driver\nclassifier. Ordered operations now represent delete then reinsert in one\nbatch. The model snapshots origin at batch start, consumes it for later\ntransactions, and derives expected rows independently of production caches.\n\nThe fixed witness reaches the real sync path with delete then reinsert of an\nexisting key. A neighboring history adds a later same-key transaction. Public\norigin at successful settlement is local in the atomic-only case and remote\nafter the later transaction. A temporary production mutant that omitted a\ndelete from the batch-local attribution set failed the atomic-only witness at\nsettlement, while the later-transaction control passed. The ordered grammar\nrejects mixed `operations` with `rows`/`deletes` and more than one copy. Other\nmixed operation sequences are representable but not exhaustively sampled.\n\n### L4. Successful overlapping mutations preserve one key grant (F08)\n\nAuthority: queued source attribution is by key and timing, not by source\nauthor or one grant per optimistic transaction. If two same-key mutations are\nboth persisting before a source transaction queues, a successful mutation\nretains one attribution for the key even if its sibling fails. Two failures\nretain none. The first same-key source transaction consumes that grant; a\nsecond source transaction is remote without another local owner.\n\nThe model already represented multiple persisting transactions but had no\ndistinguishing overlap campaign. Sixteen bounded histories now cross the two\noutcomes, settlement order, and one/two source transactions. The core driver\ncompares public origin at each settlement, full event cuts, and downstream\nrows. A temporary production mutant clearing `pendingLocalOrigins` whenever a\nfailed sibling remained failed four one-success/one-source cells at the\nsettlement read, while two-failure controls passed. The tested histories do\nnot include pending plus persisting overlap, multiple keys, truncate during\noverlap, or all possible handler schedules. The coverage map retains those\nlimits; no universal overlap proof is claimed.\n\n### L5. Real SQLite receives source updates after refusal (F05, F07)\n\nAuthority: accepted source transactions must reach the Collection and its\ndurable persisted replica; a rejected local insert contributes no durable row.\nThe real SQLite witness holds outbound rejection, accepts a same-key delete,\nthen follows one of two histories. The continuation now inserts, deletes,\nreinserts, and updates through `SyncConfig.write`. It checks live and exposed\nbase rows, `applyCommittedTx`'s update mutation, durable `loadSubset`, and a\nfresh Collection/adapter/driver reopened over the same file. A temporary\nadapter mutant dropped only the final update before durable application. It\nreached the new update cut and failed the durable-row comparison: SQLite kept\n`later source row` instead of `updated source row`. The original broad mutant\nalso dropped an earlier source turn and failed too early; that result is not\ncounted as update calibration. The receiving witness uses a SQLite CLI driver\nand a same-process reopen; other hosts and a process restart remain outside.\n\n## Oracle-guide check\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 authority and limits | L1–L5 name the contract and finite path limits. The private bridge remains unknown. |\n| ORC-002 independent judgment | The model derives rows and origin from source actions and key ownership, without production caches or classifiers. The shared `sourceOperations` function only expands grammar order. |\n| ORC-003 responsibilities | Opening prose states laws and limits; `HistoryModel`, `OptimisticStep`, the real Collection driver, and `check()` keep model, grammar, path, and refinement distinct. |\n| ORC-004 grammar controls | Pending versus persisting, one versus two queued source transactions, batch versus transaction boundary, and both settlement orders have reconstructing witnesses. Bounded domains are above. The driver rejects an ordered batch mixed with legacy rows/deletes or multiple copies, and a reentrant trigger without truncate. Other legal schedules remain listed. |\n| ORC-005 path and observation | Public Collection writes, sync callbacks, subscription replicas, downstream rows, receipts, and settlement-time reads reach the named cuts. The real adapter path checks durable and reopened rows. |\n| ORC-006 calibration | The original production code and four targeted hostile edits fail assertions at the intended public cuts. The too-broad SQLite edit failed early and is excluded from the update claim. No timeout or setup error is counted as a kill. |\n| ORC-007 fixed/random parity | Not triggered by the new bounded enumerations and fixed histories. Existing generated campaigns in the owner retain their prior configuration. |\n| ORC-008 model minimality | Pending differs from persisting because sync applies now versus waits. Source operation order and a combined reentrant drain change legal next observations. |\n| ORC-009 vocabulary | `pending`, `persisting`, sync transaction, publication, and settlement match the glossary. The model-only `row` action combines source insert/update as stated in the opening. |\n| ORC-010 failure fidelity | The core owner keeps its primary failure through cleanup. The SQLite owners now share cleanup aggregation; the persisted owner still bounds each stage, and the real witness releases Collections before directory removal. |\n| ORC-011 second formulation | Atomic delete/reinsert versus a later transaction challenges batch-boundary semantics. Causal source authorship cannot be independently decided without a source identity signal; no such guarantee is claimed. |\n| ORC-012 review evidence | This record binds original head, executable revision, RED/GREEN, mutants, finite closure, and remaining cells. |\n| ORC-013 distinguishing boundary | One versus two source transactions, one versus two reentrant suffix batches, pending versus persisting, and one/two overlapping outcomes distinguish plausible wrong timing rules at public cuts. |\n| ORC-014 controlled handoff | The real SQLite receiver tests the controlled refused-insert and source-update premise. The core reentrant history is a core claim, not claimed as real-provider coverage. |\n\n## Verification and limits\n\nOn the executable revision, 166 core optimistic history and state-retention\ntests passed; the commit-hook rerun passed 61 core publication and outcome tests.\nThe relevant persisted and real SQLite run passed 18 tests, with unrelated\npersisted cases filtered. Vitest's test type checks passed. Prettier and the\nchanged-file ESLint run had no errors; ESLint reported pre-existing warnings\nin the large persisted oracle, and one new shadow warning was repaired before\ncommit. Git's commit hook ran ESLint fixes; the two focused suites were rerun\nafter the commit. The production diff in `state.ts` is seven added and six\nremoved lines, including the corrected comment.\n\nThe issue #2071 bridge sequence is still unavailable. This review does not\nclaim to close its private path, every overlap schedule, every mixed source\nbatch, another SQLite host, or process restart. The optimistic-history owner\ncan represent mixed source order and bounded reentry; a bridge-specific\ncounterexample needs the callback/write sequence and a persisted-wrapper\nreceiving witness. The coverage map names those limits. No authorized in-scope\nrepair from these eight findings is left open.\n\nFinal accounting: **8 raw items = 8 fixed-now**. F07 and F08 include the\ntechnical qualifications recorded above; neither was dropped from the ledger.\n\n## Post-audit CI and review follow-ups\n\nThe full `db-2` CI shard on `00fcf0f` passed 5,901 runtime tests but reported a\ntest type error at `optimistic-history-oracle.ts:987`: the new manual\n`Transaction<HistoryRow>` was missing from the driver's transaction union.\nCommit `9cb0e9998ba7fc4f8bb67f2ddc6c45e58717c24b` adds that type. The\npackage test TypeScript check and 42 origin-publication tests, including their\nVitest type check, pass locally. This change does not alter the model or\nproduction behavior.\n\nCodeRabbit then reviewed `00fcf0f` and posted two actionable documentation\ncomments. Both were correct. The earlier issue #2071 review record used present\ntense for the source-batch grammar's former delete/reinsert limit; its affected\nstatements now name the historical commit and point to the ordered-batch witness\nadded in this PR. The generated `VirtualRowProps` and `VirtualOrigin` reference\npages linked to old source line numbers; all seven declaration links now match\n`virtual-props.ts`. These follow-ups do not change the eight-item accounting\nabove. All reported CI checks passed on `e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3`.\n\nCodeRabbit's next review, [5459093045](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5459093045),\nchecked that head and found an oracle harness defect. Its three findings across\nboth reviews are accounted for separately from the user's eight findings:\n\n| ID | Claim | Verdict and evidence | PR action and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR1 | The earlier #2071 review record still claimed that delete then reinsert was outside the source-batch grammar. | Correct on `00fcf0f`; the ordered grammar and coverage-map witness existed by that commit. | **fixed-now** in `e659d413e`; historical wording now identifies the earlier limit and later witness. |\n| CR2 | Six `VirtualRowProps` anchors and one `VirtualOrigin` anchor pointed into source comments. | Correct on `00fcf0f`; all seven current anchors match the declarations at lines 74, 85, 104, 133, 141, 149, and 36. | **fixed-now** in `e659d413e`; generated reference links were corrected. |\n| CR3 | A rejected pending manual transaction never starts its handler, so its `isPersisted` promise and oracle cleanup can remain pending. Commit the transaction after rejecting its handler promise. | Correct on `e659d413e`. Two source-row histories and one later-edit history failed at the settlement row assertion after the test first made cleanup safe. The proposed single-line fix alone leaves a later-edit history pending because the shared `starting` variable points to the later request. | **fixed-now** in this follow-up: commit the rejected manual request, bind each manual handler to its own deferred result, and release any still-active transaction during cleanup. The primary oracle and coverage map retain the law and limits. |\n\nThe reviewer's three findings were accurate and focused. The proposed fix for\nCR3 identified the missing commit but did not account for a later edit replacing\nthe shared deferred promise. The reviews distinguished documentation defects\nfrom a test-integrity defect and had little noise. Adjacent interleavings\nstill need checking before accepting the proposed patch verbatim.\n\n### CR3 law, reach, and calibration\n\nA pending manual optimistic transaction starts its mutation function only when\ncommitted. Handler rejection must roll it back, reject `isPersisted` with the\nsame reason, and expose the applied source row after dropping any optimistic\noverlay. This follows from the optimistic transaction and settlement entries in\nthe glossary and from `Transaction.commit()`/`rollback()`. The production path\nis `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })` → `mutate()` → `commit()` → the\ncontrolled handler promise → `isPersisted`. The model already represented\npending, persisting, and failed transactions. Its grammar could select a\nrejection but the pending fixed witnesses selected only success or direct\nrollback. The driver did not commit on rejection, and cleanup tried rollback\nonly when the expected result was still pending.\n\nThe expanded fixed matrix crosses optimistic visibility with success, direct\nrollback, and handler rejection after a same-key source insert. A second pair\nstarts a later disjoint edit before the manual request succeeds or rejects.\nAfter each step, the driver checks public rows, origin and pending-write\nmetadata, subscriber replicas, downstream rows, request outcome and reason,\nand publication cuts. At settlement it also checks the rows captured when\n`isPersisted` settles. The source-row and later-edit rejection cases failed at\nthat settlement assertion on the reviewed harness. They were assertion\nfailures, not Vitest timeouts; cleanup preserved the primary mismatch and\nreleased the pending transaction. With the commit fix in place, temporarily\nrestoring the shared handler made both later-edit cases fail at the same\nassertion. Binding the manual handler to its own deferred result makes all\neight targeted cases pass. This calibrates both the original omission and the\nplausible one-line repair that misses an adjacent legal history.\n\nThis establishes the bounded law for the named manual histories through the\ncore Collection path and public settlement, row, and publication checkpoints.\nIt does not prove longer manual interleavings, same-key cascades, or a persisted\nadapter path. The coverage map keeps those limits under the optimistic history\nowner. CR3 changes the oracle driver and its fixed histories, not the product\nimplementation or model state.\n\n| Guide requirement | CR3 evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001/003 | The oracle's opening states the manual settlement law and limits; the model, grammar, driver, and per-step comparison remain distinct. |\n| ORC-002 | The reference transition remains independent of transaction implementation; no expected state was copied from the new driver. |\n| ORC-004/007 | The new cases are bounded fixed histories; generated grammar and fixed/random campaigns did not change. |\n| ORC-005/006 | Real core transactions reach `isPersisted` and public rows. Original and shared-handler variants fail at the intended settlement assertion; the repaired driver passes. |\n| ORC-008/009 | Model state did not change. `pending`, `persisting`, `failed`, and settlement retain glossary meanings. |\n| ORC-010 | Cleanup rolls back a still-active transaction regardless of expected outcome; the original assertion remains the aggregate cause, with secondary diagnostics retained. The deferred rejection has a handler before a manual commit starts. |\n| ORC-011 | No plausible shared semantic fault between production and model was identified; this review concerned the production driver's ability to reach the modeled outcome. |\n| ORC-012/013 | This record binds the reviewed head, RED/GREEN cuts, hostile variant, neighboring success/rollback cases, and remaining limits. |\n| ORC-014 | The claim is limited to the core Collection path and makes no real-provider handoff claim. |\n\nFinal CodeRabbit accounting: **3 raw findings = 3 fixed-now**. There are no\ndeferred or design-decision items. The original user's eight-item audit remains\n**8 raw = 8 fixed-now**; the unknown private #2071 bridge sequence remains an\nevidence limit, not a CodeRabbit item.\n\n## Subsequent nine-finding review of `e659d413e`\n\nThe external medium-effort review examined `e659d413e0f52fa0f9d800fb1d5a41ca784199c3`\nby reading code; it reported no executed checks. Evaluation began on\n`ee21b1797eb7cfc9dfa18a05637477a10de67828`, which had already repaired\nthe manual-transaction defects in M04 and M05. The nine numbered findings are\nall the review's claims; its closing remarks add context, not a tenth item.\n\n| ID | Review claim and proposed repair | Technical verdict and evidence | PR disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| M01 | A truncate on key 1 clears active attribution for untouched key 2; a later key 2 source transaction in the same drain becomes remote. | **Correct, high severity.** A real subscriber committed a truncate suffix and later key 2 write while an edit on key 2 remained active. The public row was remote where the independent model required local. Both publication and settlement variants failed on the reviewed production logic. | **fixed-now.** Preserve active keys across truncate; the primary optimistic-history oracle owns the origin law. |\n| M02 | Clearing `pendingLocalChanges` on truncate caused M01; keep it and remove the temporary origin snapshots. | **Correct.** The snapshot copies covered only the truncate transaction. Removing the clear and snapshots preserves untouched active keys while existing same-key controls still consume attribution. | **fixed-now.** The production change removes the per-transaction snapshots and clears only row origins, completed attribution, and the virtual-props cache at truncate. |\n| M03 | The reentrant grammar never wrote a later different key with an active edit. | **Correct coverage gap.** The former fixed histories wrote key 1 only. The new key 2 history failed on the original production logic at the promised public cuts. | **fixed-now.** Two optimistic-visibility variants join the primary publication owner; the coverage map names the bounded shape. |\n| M04 | A pending manual transaction's shared handler could take a later edit's deferred result. | **Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed** in `ee21b1797`. The prior review record documents a later-edit RED settlement assertion and a hostile shared-handler variant; the current driver binds its own deferred. | **already-fixed.** Preserve the later-edit witness and settlement law in the optimistic-history owner. |\n| M05 | Rejection of a pending manual request never commits it, so the model fails it while production stays pending. | **Correct at the reviewed head; already fixed** in `ee21b1797`. The prior RED assertion and current guarded rejection, commit, and cleanup are recorded above. | **already-fixed.** Preserve success, direct rollback, and rejection cases; no second patch to the handler was needed. |\n| M06 | The no-cascade model comment is false for a failed same-key edit and pending manual peer. | **Correct.** Explicit cascading rollback and handler rejection made production reject the peer while the old model kept its overlay. The mismatch reached settlement, not a timeout. | **fixed-now.** The model cancels pending same-key peers; six cases distinguish same/different keys, cascading/secondary rollback, and handler rejection. |\n| M07 | The real SQLite test rejects an outbound promise that no handler observes if setup fails before insert. | **Correct.** A temporary pre-insert failure produced both the intended error and a Vitest unhandled rejection. The same injection after the guard preserved only the primary failure. | **fixed-now.** Reject outbound only once a transaction exists; this protects test failure fidelity. |\n| M08 | The model keeps active keys through truncate while production clears them, without explaining the difference. | **Correct explanatory and implementation gap.** The model's active-key rule was the independent expected behavior that exposed M01; its comment did not distinguish active from completed attribution. | **fixed-now.** Opening and local model prose now explain why untouched active keys survive and completed one-use attribution ends. |\n| M09 | The origin contract is repeated across source, glossary, guide, and generated reference pages and had drifted. | **Correct.** The same detailed paragraph appeared six times. The generated reference copy also stated the broken drain rule. | **fixed-now.** `VirtualOrigin` source prose is the detailed contract; the interface, glossary, and guide summarize and link to its generated reference entry. Five affected reference pages carry the regenerated text and corrected source anchors. |\n\nNo original severity labels were supplied. M01 is the release-relevant product\nregression; M03, M04, M05, M06, and M08 concern oracle reach or judgment; M07\nconcerns test failure fidelity; M09 concerns contract drift. The reviewer was\ntechnically accurate on all nine items at the reviewed commit and found the\ninteraction between the prior fix and active-key ownership. The proposed M02\nrepair was both correct and smaller than the snapshot approach. The review\ndid not report execution, so its failure claims needed the probes above.\n\n### Laws, reachable histories, and enforcement\n\n**L6: Truncate preserves active attribution for untouched keys (M01–M03,\nM08–M09).** The public `VirtualOrigin` contract and the existing key-and-timing\nmodel require a source write on an active local mutation's key to be local\nuntil a source operation consumes that key. A truncate ends completed one-use\nattribution, but an active mutation on a key the truncate omits is still active.\nThe old production clear contradicted this law. The old oracle lacked the\ndifferent-key suffix, so it never distinguished the clear from the model.\nThe independent model retains active keys until a source operation touches\nthem and clears completed grants at truncate. The legal fixed history has one\nactive edit on key 2, a subscriber-triggering truncate, a second truncate\nwriting key 1, then a non-truncate key 2 source transaction in the same drain.\nBoth optimistic and nonoptimistic edit visibility are tested. The existing\nlater same-key suffix controls the opposite boundary: a key written by the\ntruncate loses attribution for a later transaction. The real Collection sync\ndriver compares public rows and origin, both subscriber replicas, downstream\nrows, outcomes, and complete publication cuts after each step. On the old\nimplementation, the nonoptimistic case failed at source publication and the\noptimistic case failed when the failed edit's `isPersisted` settled: actual\n`remote`, expected `local`. Those are assertion failures at the intended\ncuts. The repaired code passes both and the same-key controls. This closes the\ntwo-key, one-active-edit, two-suffix-batch core boundary tested here; it does\nnot establish every longer reentrant drain or provider bridge sequence.\n\n**L7: A cascading failure cancels a pending same-key peer (M06).** Public\ntransaction settlement and the established same-key conflict rule require a\npending manual peer to reject when a failing edit cascades rollback to it.\nA different-key peer, an already persisting peer, or a secondary rollback\ndoes not acquire that outcome from this failure. The old model comment and\ntransition wrongly omitted the pending-peer cancellation, allowing false\nfailures against correct production behavior. The model now uses its own\none-Collection key equality and pending state to derive the canceled peers;\nit does not import the production conflict helper. The six fixed histories\ncross same/different keys with direct cascading rollback, secondary rollback,\nand handler rejection. They run real `createTransaction({ autoCommit: false })`,\n`mutate`, `commit` or `rollback`, and `isPersisted`. Each step compares\nvisible rows, origin, event replicas, downstream rows, request outcomes, and\npublication cuts. The old no-cascade model failed at settlement in the\nsame-key cases; the corrected model passes all six, while the different-key\nand secondary controls distinguish overbroad cancellation. This closes the\ntwo-request pending-peer boundary tested here, not arbitrary conflict graphs.\n\n**L8: A pending manual request settles by its own handler (M04–M05).** The\ncontract, grammar, core path, settlement observations, original RED results,\nshared-handler hostile control, and remaining longer-history limit are\ndocumented under CR3 above. This review rechecked that the current driver\nstill uses that path and that its focused suite remains green. M04 and M05\nwere true of `e659d413e` but required no new repair after `ee21b1797`.\n\nM07 needed a controlled failure injection, not a product model: the test\ncleanup must not create an unhandled rejection that masks its primary setup\nfailure. M09 needed direct source comparison, TypeDoc output, and working\nrelative links, not a state oracle. The five generated reference pages contain\none detailed contract in `VirtualOrigin` and summaries elsewhere; their\nsource anchors match the current declarations.\n\n### Oracle-guide audit and verification\n\n| Requirement | Outcome for this review |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001/003 | L6–L8 state authority, finite limits, model rules, grammar, production path, and comparison cuts beside the executable owner. The canonical origin prose and coverage map were updated with the executable law. |\n| ORC-002/008/009 | The model retains active keys independently of production tracking and distinguishes pending from persisting. Cancellation uses model key/state, not the production helper. The model-only row action and public terms remain mapped in the opening. |\n| ORC-004/013 | The new key 2 suffix reconstructs the reported trace; same-key later-write and both visibility modes distinguish wrong boundaries. The six peer histories ablate key equality and cascade mode. The existing grammar rejects a reentrant suffix without truncate. Domains and longer-history limits are explicit above and in the coverage map. |\n| ORC-005/006 | Original truncate logic and old no-cascade model failed assertions at named public cuts. The earlier manual-handler omissions and shared-handler mutant failed at settlement. No timeout, setup error, or mere path reach is counted as a kill. |\n| ORC-007 | The additions are bounded fixed histories, not a new important generated property. Existing generated campaigns were not changed. |\n| ORC-010 | The real SQLite pre-insert injection separated its primary error from the unhandled rejection; the guard removed the latter. Existing cleanup aggregation still preserves primary history failures. |\n| ORC-011 | No new shared semantic fault between production and the independent model was identified, so a second formulation is not triggered. Same-key and different-key controls test the boundary; no new real-provider equivalence is claimed. |\n| ORC-012/014 | This record binds the reviewed head and RED/GREEN evidence. The core reentrant claim stays at the core Collection boundary; the private bridge still needs its callback/write sequence and a persisted receiver. |\n\nAfter the repair, 241 core tests passed across optimistic publication,\noutcomes, state retention, and truncate readiness, with no test type errors.\nThe SQLite real-adapter and persisted oracle run passed 869 tests with two\nexisting todo tests and no test type errors. The doc generator emitted the five\nrelevant reference pages, but also produced unrelated changes because several\npackage dependencies were unavailable; those unrelated changes were not part\nof this repair. The focused source and link checks found no stale repeated\norigin paragraph or invalid `VirtualOrigin` relative link.\n\nFinal loss audit for this review: **9 raw items = 7 fixed-now + 2\nalready-fixed**. There are no refutations, accepted-design items, design\ndecisions, or agreed deferrals. The reviewed claims are accounted for and\nthe authorized bounded repairs are complete. No universal claim is made for\nlonger reentrant suffixes, larger rollback conflict graphs, other SQLite\nhosts, or the unavailable private #2071 bridge sequence; their executable\nowners and needed witnesses remain in the coverage map.\n\n### CodeRabbit review of `ee21b1797`\n\n[Review 5460095613](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460095613)\ncontained one outside-diff behavior suggestion and one inline documentation\nsuggestion. Both are accounted for here; its suggested agent prompts and local\ntool invocation are review metadata, not additional findings.\n\n| ID | Claim and proposed action | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR4 | The model keeps `activeKeys` after an empty truncate while production clears its active-key tracking; clear `activeKeys` after taking the batch snapshot. | Production did clear active tracking on the reviewed `ee21b1797`, so the observation was accurate there. Its proposed model change contradicts the `VirtualOrigin` active-key law and would make the model accept M01's product regression. After the production repair at `a109687c1`, that change was run as a temporary model mutant. Both new key 2 cases failed at their intended public cuts: the nonoptimistic case at complete source publication and the optimistic case at `isPersisted` settlement. The mutant was removed. | **stale** as a production/model mismatch after M01's repair; **refuted** as a model repair. Keep the empty-truncate/different-key challenge under the optimistic-history owner. |\n| CR5 | Remove a hiring recommendation from the engineering review record. | Correct maintainability point: the durable record needs code and test evidence; the user-facing review can carry the requested reviewer assessment. | **fixed-now.** Removed the personnel recommendation from this record while retaining the technical quality assessment and all law evidence. |\n\nCodeRabbit accounting for this review: **2 raw items = 1 stale with a refuted\nrepair + 1 fixed-now**. CR4's historical observation and useful adversarial\nhistory remain recorded; no product law or test coverage was weakened to\naccept the proposed model change.\n\n### Merge-readiness self-review after CI passed on `7dcebc75f`\n\nThe public origin prose said that a later same-key transaction after a\ntruncate is remote without specifying the drain boundary. The existing\nreentrant witness has a trigger and suffix truncate in separate drains. The\nstill-active mutation can attribute the suffix truncate again. A later\nsame-key transaction within the suffix drain is remote. The source comment,\ngenerated reference, oracle opening, publication comment, and coverage map\nnow state that narrower rule. This is a contract wording correction, not a\nproduction change.\n\nTwo temporary legal-history probes also crossed a pending and persisting\nsame-key pair with a reentrant truncate, and two active keys with that drain.\nBoth passed the core public-history comparison and test type check. The probes\nwere removed. They support those two sampled schedules but do not close the\ncoverage map's longer overlap or provider-path limits.\n\n### CodeRabbit review of `c7d99a43f`\n\n[Review 5460769310](https://github.com/TanStack/db/pull/2078#pullrequestreview-5460769310)\ncontained one inline documentation finding. The review body also suggested\nrunning the local CodeRabbit CLI after a fix. Both items are accounted for\nbelow.\n\n| ID | Claim and proposed action | Evidence and technical verdict | Disposition and durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CR6 | The “after green CI” heading implied the current head had passed; change it to say CI was pending. | The preceding head `7dcebc75f` had passed its 26 checks, but checks for reviewed head `c7d99a43f` were still pending at review time. The unqualified heading was ambiguous. | **fixed-now.** The heading now names the head whose CI had passed, preserving the historical sequence without implying current-head success. |\n| CR7 | Consider running `coderabbit review --agent` after the fix. | This was an optional workflow prompt, not a code or contract finding. The hosted review already examined `c7d99a43f`; the local CLI would add no required evidence for this wording change. | **refuted** as an additional PR requirement. Keep the hosted review and CI status as the review evidence. |\n\nCodeRabbit accounting for this review: **2 raw items = 1 fixed-now + 1\nrefuted**. This documentation finding affects no product law or oracle path;\nits evidence is the recorded commit order and CI status at review time.\n"}
